# The accessor of a constant whose value a header holds, and what stands
# around it (design.md §4.19; panel 038; CLAUDE.md §7). Split out of
# `emit/body.hero` on 2026-10-10, when defects 570 and 571 gave the accessor
# lines of its own and that file passed §11's 300 lines: a body has a
# prologue and blocks, an accessor has neither.
#
# **The accessor is the compiler's line among the program's**, the one place
# in the definitions where a group's macro is expanded on purpose: its
# `return NAME;` (or, for a record's constant, the initialisation
# `emit/record_constant.hero` writes) names the header's value. A header's
# deprecations are quiet over the whole unit since panel 208
# (`emit/deprecation.hero`), so none is said here; every check of a binding
# is raised again after it, since a `_Pragma` in the value reaches every line
# below (`emit/checks_raised.hero`, defect 570). A constant of the
# compiler's own headers, the library's `HERO_OS_OK` among them, is the
# runtime's, and is written with neither.

use ast
use source
use check/state
use ir/containers
use emit/ctype
use emit/signature
use emit/record_constant
use emit/checks_raised
use emit/value_check
use emit/writer
use emit/externs as emit_externs
use parse
use resolve
use checker
use ir/lower
use cli/strings

# The accessor, its signature at the declaration as every signature is, and
# its `return` at the constant's own line too (defect 2068): a `step` into the
# constant and a breakpoint on its line stop at the return's instruction, which
# under the generated file's `#line` showed the generated C and left the
# breakpoint with no location. The `}` stays the generated file's, the exit
# being the compiler's (defect 335). WHAT MOVED WITH IT, measured before it
# landed over the 70 programs of the tree that declare a header's constant and
# 33 planted mistakes of a header's value, on this Mac and on Linux arm64's
# clang 22.1.8: no diagnostic of the compiler's and no exit code; a clang line
# about the value itself, a warning of its arithmetic or an error inside a
# value C cannot parse, now names this line where it named the generated file,
# and `emit/ffi_site.hero`'s gate, which asks the location, finds no reader
# that claims it.
function accessor(@w: writer.Writer, f: containers.Function, c: state.Checked, names: ctype.Names, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl])
    writer.at_span(@w, s, offset: f.span.start)

    # A record's constant is built from the header's value, which may be a
    # brace list that `return` cannot take (defect 094).
    if record_constant.is_record_value(c, decls, f)
        record_constant.accessor(@w, f, c, names, s, decls)
    else
        writer.line(@w, text: signature.signature(f, c, names, s, decls) + " {")

        # An integer's value held to C's terms where it is read: a value C
        # leaves undefined is an error at this line (`emit/value_check.hero`,
        # defect 2072).
        held = value_check.asked(c, s, decls, f)

        if held
            value_check.opened(@w)
        writer.at_span(@w, s, offset: f.span.start)
        writer.line(@w, text: "    return " + source.span_text(s, decls[f.decl].name) + ";")
        writer.at_generated(@w)

        if held
            writer.line(@w, text: value_check.POP)
        writer.line(@w, text: "}")

    checks_raised.after_constant(@w, s, decls, f)

## Tests

# A header's constant, a number's and a handle's alike, returns on its own
# `constant` line and closes on the generated file (defect 2068); a record's
# keeps `emit/record_constant.hero`'s accessor.
test "a header's constant returns on its own line, and closes on the generated file"
    text = "extern \"cinit.h\"\n    record Db tag sqlite3\n    constant NO_DB: Db\n    constant LIMIT: i64\n\nfunction main()\n    print(NO_DB == NO_DB)\n    print(LIMIT)\n"
    parsed = parse.parse(text)
    s = source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: text)
    out = resolve.resolve(parsed.tree, s, parsed.decls, parsed.uses)
    c: state.Checked = checker.check(parsed.tree, out, s, parsed.decls)
    assert c.diagnostics.len() == 0
    low = lower.lower(parsed.tree, out, c, s, parsed.decls)
    names = ctype.new_names(s, parsed.decls)
    written: [str] @= []

    for f in low.program.functions
        if emit_externs.is_extern_constant(parsed.decls, f)
            w: writer.Writer @= writer.new_writer("t")
            accessor(@w, f, c, names, s, parsed.decls)
            written @ written.push(writer.finish(w))

    got = written.join("")
    assert written.len() == 2
    assert strings.holds(text: got, needle: "#line 3 \"t.hero\"\n    return NO_DB;\n#line ")
    assert strings.holds(text: got, needle: "#line 4 \"t.hero\"\n    return LIMIT;\n#line ")
    assert strings.holds(text: got, needle: " \"t.c\"\n}\n")
